  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 307 PM EDT Thursday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Sfc analysis indicates a rather messy scenario acrs our \ncwa this aftn with pre-frontal trof/warm frnt lifting acrs the \nnorthern Dacks into southern VT, while first cold frnt is \napproaching the eastern Great Lakes and secondary cold frnt is \nlocated over the northern Great Lakes. This piece meal style \nscenario limits sfc convergence and displaces strongest \nforcing/shear with best instability, resulting in mostly strong to \nvery marginally severe storms thru this evening. Given \nthermodynamics and wind profiles, a few stronger storms are possible \nwith localized gusty winds as the primary threat. RAP analysis shows \nsfc based CAPE values in the 1200-1800 J/kg ahead of initial broken \nline of showers with embedded storms over the CPV, while secondary \ninstability maximum over the SLV with values near 2000 J/kg ahead of \ncold frnt. Meanwhile, the 0-6km deep layer shear is in the 25 to 35 \nknot range with greatest values near the International Border per \nlatest analysis. Bottom line, a few stronger storms wl be possible \nthru sunset this evening acrs our cwa. Did coordinate with SPC to \nkeep SVR Thunderstorm Watch #631 to the south and west of our cwa \nthis evening. \n\nGOES-19 mid lvl water vapor imagery shows a large area of dry air \nacrs the Ohio Valley into the Great Lakes, which is shifting toward \nour cwa and should limit flash flood potential. The only place to \nwatch would be central/southern VT, where low lvl flow parallels \nmid/upper lvl flow as mid/upper lvl trof approaches, but overall \nsystem is progress and flash flood potential is minimal. PW analysis \nshows values near 1.5\" this evening ahead of boundary, but as \nmid/upper lvl trof and associated dry slot develops by Friday \nmorning, values fall to 1.0\" or less. \n\nAfter midnight fcst challenge becomes fog/mist potential as strong \nsubsidence/dry air aloft builds overhead. Given upstream satl \nimagery feel stratus clouds are likely that could transition into \nlocalized patchy fog, especially northern NY valleys. Also, areas \nthat receive rainfall within a few hours of sunset wl have a higher \nprobability of fog, especially CT River Valley and parts of central \nVT. Temps drop back into the mid 50s to lower 60s by Friday evening.\n\nA few additional showers with embedded storms are possible on \nFriday, especially acrs central and northern VT. Have continued with \nhigh chc pops ahead of secondary cold frnt thru 18z, before much \ndrier air develops by mid aftn. Temps range from the mid 70s to near \n80F acrs our cwa. High pres builds into our cwa on Friday night with \nbetter probability of fog as temps drop below cross over values \nunder clear skies and light winds. Lows mid 30s to mid 50s, warmest \nnear Lake Champlain and coolest SLK. Saturday is quiet with high \npres overhead and comfortable temps/humidities.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: We expected an active weather pattern across Vermont and \nnorthern New York for much of next week as we remain under the \ninfluence of northwesterly flow aloft. Deep layer ridging will exist \nacross the central US with general cyclonic/northwesterly flow aloft \nwhich will help usher in a series of disturbances throughout the \nupcoming week. This should help keep temperatures a few degrees \nbelow seasonal normals with plentiful cloud cover. The good news, \nhowever, is that no individual day looks like a wash-out at this \ntime but it'll be worth watching for those with outdoor activities \nplanned next week.",
